+## 2026-11-16 TASK-063: the guard's threat model drawn after round 9, its deliberate reaches recorded (R-082)
+
+- **Question:** whether round 9's findings that reach a refused action by a program that runs what it is handed send the work back. The security audit failed round 9 on ssh's `-o` rule stepped round (a getopt cluster, a leading blank, a quoted key, scp's and sftp's `-o`) and a locale value with a modifier ending `.utf8`; Review A named the same ssh forms and a `/bin/../` path to Git's launcher, beside two findings of another kind (B1, a regression of round 9 in the walk's time; B2, `NAME+=value` read as the command word). Rounds 5 to 9 had each graded such reaches blocking, and each round's audit had found the next program.
+- **Decision: B1 and B2 go to round 10; the rest is outside the guard's threat model, recorded in docs/HARDENING.md under TASK-063 and never blocking by itself.** The guard's threat-model row (docs/INVENTORY.md, Decisions), owed since round 1's brief, is written: a command that names a refused action in its own words, under any quoting, chain, wrapper shell, prefix, assignment or launcher the guard reads or sees through, and the guard's deadline, are inside; a deliberate reach that hides the action from that reading (code given to an interpreter, a command a program takes from an option, its configuration or the environment, a word a child program parses again under a runtime or locale crafted for it, a path written to reach an excluded launcher) is outside. The refusals already built against some of those stand as defence in depth.
+- **Reasoning.** `python -c` with any payload passes the guard by design (the second resolution round left interpreters outside the increment), and an agent set on running a refused action gains nothing from ssh's `ProxyCommand` or a Big5 locale that `python -c` does not give it: the guard holds the commands an agent types by habit, by mistake or in a shape variant, and the agents run as the owner's Windows user with no isolation (KP-032). The bridge's whole threat model (the row of 2026-11-13) already puts a deliberate reach by a process running as the owner's account outside the five; the guard's row narrows it for the guard, as the rows of the review guard, the strip and the launcher's target rule do for theirs. The supervisor's probe showed the family open on every tree (`GIT_SSH_COMMAND`, `git -c core.sshCommand`, scp `-S`, sftp `-D`, tar's checkpoint action), so another round of programs would have met the same audit again. B2 is inside (the action in its own words, an assignment misread, against R-082's reading as the shell does); B1 is the deadline, the increment's own safety mechanism, regressed by round 9's fix: it could not lift a round, so round 10 was lifted by B2's class line and its brief had the pre-launch read the first fix-induced regression asks for. Told to the owner as a change they can stop (rule 58).
+
+## 2026-11-15 TASK-063: second resolution round, after four rounds and a third that regressed the permission guard (R-082)
+
+- **Question** (handoff/RESOLUTION-TASK-063-2.md): how the guard's reader is built so that it converges. Round 3 of the first resolution round's subset ran to the launcher's two-hour limit with 30 tests failing; round 4, lifted by a class line, made the 30 green (968 passed, 20 skipped) and was rejected by Review A (seventeen blocking findings) and the security audit (ten), among them regressions of refusals round 3 or fae0860 made: an assignment before a chain crashing the hook in a Workspace (exit 1, fail-open), the brace group dropped, rule 8 missing a newline list inside a subshell, a backslash-newline inside double quotes no longer joined, a here-document piped to a shell. The third round that regressed something already passing (rounds 1, 2 and 4).
